# =============================================================================
# os8088 - apps/arcade/qbert.patch - what Q*bert's own 8088 code needs changed
# to run NATIVELY in a segment of the PC (ARCADE-SPEC 10.2)
#
# ADDRESSES ONLY, never a ROM byte: apps/arcade/arcqbgfx.py applies each to
# the program image and REFUSES unless the instruction there has the shape
# the kind names (the opcode and the operand it changes), so a ROM that is
# not the one these were found in fails the build instead of running wrong.
#
#   nop ADDR N    N bytes at ADDR become NOP: the reset's segment loads,
#                 so the program keeps the segment the host gave it
#   port ADDR     a WRITE to 5800-5807h (the watchdog, the sound latch, the
#                 video and knocker outputs) moved 8 up, to 5808-580Fh: the
#                 board reads its inputs at the same addresses, and the host
#                 fills them, so a write there must not land on them
#   yield ADDR    the wait for the next NMI (`cmp ax,[0080h] / jz $-4`)
#                 becomes `call far` into the N88 core, which hands the
#                 frame back to the host (the far pointer is the host's to
#                 fill, at power-on)
#
# Found by tools/qbnative.py's walk from the reset, the NMI handlers and a
# linear scan; its run checks that no write reaches 5800-5807h and that the
# segment registers never change (ARCADE-SPEC 10.2).
# =============================================================================
nop   B25E 6        # mov es,ax / mov ds,ax / mov ss,ax
port  AF70          # the NMI handler's watchdog
port  B034          # ...and its video outputs (priority, flip, knocker)
port  C2A8          # the sound latch: reset, then the command
port  C2AD
port  DB69          # the service mode's NMI handler: the watchdog
port  DB94          # ...its outputs and its sound
port  DB97
port  DB9C
port  DD52
port  DD57
port  EA53
port  EA5A
port  EA6E
port  EA73
yield B36D          # the game's wait for the frame
yield DB77          # the service mode's
